# Personal preferences

These instructions apply to every coding agent I use (Claude Code, Codex,
OpenCode and others). Client-specific instructions live in
`~/Developer/<client>/AGENTS.md` and add to these.

## Language

Write these instruction files in English. Talk to me, the operator, in Italian
by default: replies, questions, summaries and explanations. Use another
language only when the conversation is already in it, or for an artifact that
already uses it (code, commit messages, docs written in English).

## Environment

macOS — assume zsh, Homebrew at `/opt/homebrew`, and BSD-flavored CLI tools
(e.g. `sed -i '' …`, not GNU syntax) unless the project pins a specific
toolchain.

## Google accounts

The `google-workspace` server is the same in every profile and holds the tokens
of every Google account; pick the account with `user_google_email`. Outside
client folders use `{{ .googleAccount }}`; in a client folder use the account
named by the client's `AGENTS.md`, and the others only as that file allows.
Never start a Google login for an account whose token is missing: stop and ask.

`{{ .workMail }}` is a Fastmail account for work mail and **has no
Google Workspace account**: never use it as `user_google_email`, even when it
appears as my email in the session context.

## GitHub / SSH

GitHub authentication goes through SSH with the key held by 1Password (SSH
agent). Every operation that uses the key (`git push`, `git fetch`, `git clone`
from a `git@github.com:…` remote) needs my interactive approval in the
1Password prompt; without it the operation fails (permission denied, timeout).
That is not a configuration problem: wait for me to approve and retry.

Never work around the SSH flow to skip that approval: no switching to HTTPS
remotes, no rewriting remote URLs, no tokens or `GIT_ASKPASS`. If an SSH
operation fails for lack of approval, tell me and wait, then retry over SSH.

## Secrets

Passwords and keys from 1Password and Bitwarden go through `ai-secrets`. Put
the reference in an environment variable and run the command through it:

```sh
KEY='op://<vault>/<item>/<field>' ai-secrets run --reason "<why>" -- <command>
KEY='bw://<account>/<item>' ai-secrets run --reason "<why>" -- <command>
```

- I approve every new secret in 1Password, after a notification that shows
  `--reason`: write a reason that tells me what it is for. A value approved in
  the last 30 minutes comes back without asking.
- Never print a secret, write it to a file, put it on a command line or pass it
  to another tool: it lives only in the environment of the command run by
  `ai-secrets run`.
- Do not call `op read` or `bw get` directly, and do not unlock Bitwarden
  yourself.
- If you do not know the reference of a secret, ask me for it.

## Workflow

- Do not append `Co-Authored-By:` trailers (or "Generated with …" lines) to
  commit messages — keep them clean.
- Before rebasing, large refactors, or any multi-file restructuring,
  summarize the branch's goals and the proposed changes and wait for
  confirmation before committing.
- For non-trivial features in a codebase that already has tests, prefer the
  test-first cycle: write the failing test, then the minimum code to pass,
  then refactor. Skip on small bugfixes or one-line tweaks.
- When creating a git branch, never set its upstream to the branch it was
  started from. Create it with plain `git switch -c <name>` (no start-point
  ref like `origin/main`), and before the first push check the upstream with
  `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref @{upstream}`. If it points at another branch,
  run `git branch --unset-upstream` first. Publish with
  `git push -u origin HEAD` so the remote branch takes the same name.

## Dotfiles (chezmoi)

My dotfiles are managed by chezmoi: the source of truth is
`~/.local/share/chezmoi` (remote `git@github.com:ilmeskio/dotfiles.git`),
not the files in my home directory. Managed files include `~/.gitconfig`,
`~/.zshrc`, this file (`~/.agents/AGENTS.md`), `~/.claude/` (except
`settings.json`, which Claude Code rewrites itself), the profile links in
`~/.claude-<profile>/` and `~/Developer/<client>/AGENTS.md`.

- Never edit a managed file directly in home, and never use `git config
  --global` for a permanent setting — `chezmoi apply` will silently discard
  it. Edit the source instead: `chezmoi edit --apply <target>`, or modify the
  file under `~/.local/share/chezmoi` and then `chezmoi apply <target>`.
- `chezmoi re-add` does not work on templates (files ending in `.tmpl`, e.g.
  `dot_gitconfig.tmpl`) — it skips them without a word. Check with
  `chezmoi source-path <target>` before assuming a file can be re-added.
- Preview a template's rendered output with `chezmoi cat <target>` before
  applying it.
- Check for drift with `chezmoi status` when you touch anything managed, and
  tell me what it reports. Never run a bare `chezmoi apply` to resolve drift
  you did not create — it overwrites the home version. Show me the
  `chezmoi diff` and let me decide which side wins.

## Testing

Verify claims by actually running tests and builds before reporting status.
Never describe a test as "passing" or "verifying behavior" without executing
it in this session.

## Writing documents

**Describe the state being proposed, not how you got there.** A document is read by
people who were not in the conversation that produced it: constructions like "it
stays three because…", "the alternative was discarded", "it's no longer a fourth
thing", "until yesterday this said…" narrate an earlier, wider draft and force the
reader to reconstruct a discussion they never had. Write what is true now, in the
affirmative.

Where the roads not taken are worth keeping — because a reader would otherwise
propose them again — collect them in a section of their own at the end
(*Discarded directions*), each with the reason it does not hold. Not scattered
through the prose as negations.

The same applies to conventions: state a rule once, where it belongs. A document
that repeats what the project's README or a guidance already says spends its first
paragraphs on something the reader can find elsewhere.

## Communication style

When asked for examples or alternative approaches, present them as choices
for the user to pick from. Do not pick one silently and start editing.

Be literal in technical contexts. When a word could mean either a concrete
artifact (a git branch, a file, a temp folder, a process) or a figure of
speech (a "branch" to explore, a "thread" of reasoning), state which you
mean — never leave it ambiguous. Prefer plain, concrete wording over
metaphor when describing what exists and where it lives.

